---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Prose from "../components/Prose.astro";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import Cloud from "reicon/icons/Cloud";
import Rocket from "reicon/icons/Rocket";

const installCommand = "curl -fsSL https://cdn.coollabs.io/coolify/install.sh | sudo bash";
const internalUrl = "postgres://<username>:<password>@<database-container>:5432/<database>";
const backupCommand = "pg_dump --format=custom --no-acl --no-owner";

const faq = [
  {
    question: "Which PostgreSQL versions can I run with Coolify?",
    answer:
      "The database selector offers PostgreSQL 18, 17 and 16, plus Supabase PostgreSQL, PostGIS and PGVector extension images. PostgreSQL 18 (`postgres:18-alpine`) is the default. The list follows the current Coolify release.",
  },
  {
    question: "How does Coolify back up PostgreSQL?",
    answer:
      "A backup schedule runs `pg_dump` in custom format for the databases you select, or `pg_dumpall` with gzip when you select all databases. The file is written on the database's server and can be uploaded to S3-compatible storage.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I send backups to S3, Cloudflare R2 or Backblaze B2?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Coolify works with S3-compatible storage. The docs have setup guides for Amazon S3, Cloudflare R2 and Supabase Storage, and also list DigitalOcean Spaces, MinIO, Backblaze B2, Scaleway, Hetzner S3 Storage, Wasabi, Vultr, CloudPe and IDrive e2.",
  },
  {
    question: "How long are backups kept?",
    answer:
      "You decide. Local retention can limit the number of backups, the days to keep and the maximum storage in GB (0 means no limit). A backup is deleted when any limit is reached, and the most recent ones are kept first. S3 retention is set separately.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I restore a PostgreSQL backup?",
    answer:
      "Open Import Backup on the running database and pick a file from your computer, a path on the server or your S3 storage. Coolify detects the format, shows the exact `pg_restore` or `psql` command, and runs PostgreSQL restores in one transaction. Stop application writes and back up the target first.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does Coolify support point-in-time recovery for Postgres?",
    answer:
      "Coolify's scheduled backups are `pg_dump` and `pg_dumpall` dumps, so you restore to the moment a dump was taken. PostgreSQL documents point-in-time recovery as part of continuous WAL archiving, which is a separate setup.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is my database exposed to the internet?",
    answer:
      "Not by default. A new database has no public endpoint. Apps on the same Coolify network use the internal URL. If an outside client needs access, you can turn on a public TCP proxy or a port mapping, restrict it with a firewall and use SSL.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Self-Host PostgreSQL with Automated Backups | Coolify"
  canonicalUrl="https://coolify.io/self-host-postgres-with-backups"
  description="Run PostgreSQL on your own server with Coolify: one-click setup, scheduled backups to S3, retention and restore. What you still own, and when to go managed."
>
  <section class="px-4">
    <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
    <h1 class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance text-fg md:text-5xl">
      What's the easiest way to self-host a Postgres database with backups<span class="text-warning">?</span>
    </h1>
  </section>

  <article class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-3xl px-4 text-left">
    <Prose>
      <p class="text-base md:text-lg">
        <strong>Short answer:</strong> Install Coolify on a server you control, add PostgreSQL as a one-click database,
        and give it a backup schedule that uploads to S3-compatible storage. Coolify generates the credentials and
        connection URL, keeps the data on a persistent volume, runs <code>pg_dump</code> on your schedule and can restore
        a backup from the dashboard. You still own the server, its updates and regular restore tests.
      </p>
      <p>
        Coolify is free and open source when you self-host it. If you don't have it yet, one command installs it on a
        Linux server (see <a href="/deploy-apps-on-your-own-vps">deploying on your own VPS</a> for the full steps):
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="card mt-4 py-3 px-4">
      <code class="block overflow-x-auto font-mono text-sm whitespace-nowrap text-fg select-all">
        <span class="pr-2 text-fg-faint select-none">$</span>{installCommand}
      </code>
    </div>

    <Prose class="mt-4">
      <h2>Run PostgreSQL on your server</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Open New Resource</strong> in the project and environment where the database should live, then pick
          <strong>PostgreSQL</strong> under Databases.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Choose the server and network.</strong> Pick the server that should run Postgres and the Docker
          network destination it should join. Build servers can't host databases.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pick an image.</strong> At the time of writing the selector offers PostgreSQL 18, 17 and 16, plus
          Supabase PostgreSQL, PostGIS and PGVector extension images. PostgreSQL 18 (<code>postgres:18-alpine</code>) is
          the default.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Review the generated settings.</strong> Coolify creates the username <code>postgres</code>, the initial
          database <code>postgres</code>, a generated password and the connection URLs. Data lives on a named Docker
          volume, so recreating the container doesn't remove it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Start it</strong> and wait until it's running and healthy. A health check is attached by default.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Copy the internal URL</strong> into the environment variables of your app:
        </li>
      </ol>
    </Prose>

    <div class="card mt-2 py-3 px-4">
      <code class="block overflow-x-auto font-mono text-sm whitespace-nowrap text-fg select-all">{internalUrl}</code>
    </div>

    <Prose class="mt-4">
      <p>
        The internal URL uses the database container name and works for apps on the same Coolify network. You can also
        add initialization scripts (they run only on an empty data directory) and your own <code>postgresql.conf</code>.
        The <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/databases/postgresql">PostgreSQL docs page</a> covers every setting.
      </p>

      <h2>Private by default, public when you need it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Private:</strong> a new database gets no public endpoint. Apps on the same network connect with the
          internal URL.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Public:</strong> if a client outside the server must connect, choose <strong>Public through TCP
          proxy</strong> and set a public port. Coolify then shows a public URL as well. A direct port mapping such as
          <code>15432:5432</code> is the other option.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lock it down:</strong> the docs advise keeping database ports private whenever possible. If you open
          one, restrict it with your server or provider firewall and use SSL.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Encrypt connections with SSL</h2>
      <p>
        Coolify can generate a server certificate for the database, signed by a CA for that server. Stop the database,
        set <strong>SSL</strong> to Enabled, pick an SSL mode and restart. PostgreSQL supports <code>allow</code>,
        <code>prefer</code>, <code>require</code> (the default), <code>verify-ca</code> and <code>verify-full</code>.
        The two verify modes need the connecting app to trust the Coolify CA certificate, which you mount from
        <code>/data/coolify/ssl/coolify-ca.crt</code>. Copy the updated URL into your app afterwards. See
        <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/databases/ssl">Database SSL</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Schedule backups</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Add a schedule.</strong> Open the database, select <strong>Backups</strong>, then <strong>+ Add</strong>.
          Enter a five-field cron expression or a named schedule: <code>hourly</code>, <code>daily</code>,
          <code>weekly</code>, <code>monthly</code> and others.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Choose what to back up.</strong> Pick specific databases or all of them. For specific databases Coolify
          runs <code>{backupCommand}</code>. For all databases it runs <code>pg_dumpall</code> and gzips the output.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Set retention.</strong> Local copies are written under <code>/data/coolify/backups</code> on the
          database's server. You can limit backups to keep, days to keep and maximum storage in GB (0 means no limit).
          A backup is deleted when any limit is reached, and the most recent ones are kept first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Send a copy off the server.</strong> Add and validate an S3-compatible storage, then enable it on the
          schedule. You can delete the local copy after a successful upload. S3 retention is set separately from local
          retention.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Run it once by hand.</strong> Select <strong>Back up now</strong> and check the execution: status
          Success, the right databases, a file size above zero and, if enabled, a successful S3 upload.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>Good to know:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Storage providers.</strong> The docs have setup guides for Amazon S3, Cloudflare R2 and Supabase
          Storage, and list DigitalOcean Spaces, MinIO, Backblaze B2, Scaleway, Hetzner S3 Storage, Wasabi, Vultr,
          CloudPe and IDrive e2 as supported. The bucket must exist before you validate it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Timing.</strong> A schedule runs in the server's timezone (or the Coolify instance timezone if the server
          has none). A backup runs only while the database is running. If it's stopped when the schedule fires, that run
          is skipped. The job timeout defaults to 3600 seconds.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Alerts.</strong> Coolify can send Backup Success and Backup Failure notifications by email, Discord,
          Telegram, Slack, Pushover or webhook. A failed S3 upload after a good local backup is reported through Backup
          Failure.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Postgres inside a one-click service</strong> can be backed up too. Coolify reads
          <code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code>, <code>POSTGRES_USER</code> and <code>POSTGRES_DB</code> from the container.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Full reference: <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/databases/backups">Database backups</a>.</p>

      <h2>Restore a backup</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Stop application writes and take a separate backup of the target database.</li>
        <li>
          Open <strong>Import Backup</strong> on the running database and choose a source: a file from your computer, an
          absolute path on the server, or a file in your S3 storage.
        </li>
        <li>
          Review the detected format and the read-only <strong>Import command</strong>. Coolify picks
          <code>pg_restore</code> or <code>psql</code> to match the dump. It accepts SQL dumps, custom archives, tar
          archives and all-database dumps, compressed with gzip, bz2, xz or zip.
        </li>
        <li>
          Start the restore and follow the output. PostgreSQL restores run in one transaction. Replacing existing
          objects and keeping owners and privileges are both off by default.
        </li>
        <li>Check schemas and a few real records with a client, then turn writes back on and take a fresh backup.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Imports are also available through the API. See
        <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/databases/restore">Restore a database</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What you still own</h2>
      <p>Coolify automates the database and its backups. It doesn't take the operator job off your plate:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Server security.</strong> OS updates, firewall rules and SSH access are yours, whether you self-host
          Coolify or use Coolify Cloud. Coolify can notify you when a patch check finds OS updates.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Testing restores.</strong> In the docs' words: "A successful backup only proves that Coolify created a
          file." Restore a copy into a disposable database and check that your app can read it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Off-server copies.</strong> Local backups live on the same server as the database. Turn on S3 so a lost
          server doesn't take the backups with it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Monitoring.</strong> With Sentinel metrics enabled, Coolify shows CPU and memory history for the
          database container, and it can warn you when server disk usage crosses a threshold. Query performance and
          slow queries are up to you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Major version upgrades.</strong> Don't switch a production database image to a new major version
          without following PostgreSQL's upgrade procedure and taking a verified backup first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The Coolify instance itself.</strong> Coolify's instance backup saves projects and settings, not the
          data in your databases. Database backups are a separate job.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>When to use something else</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>You need point-in-time recovery.</strong> Coolify's scheduled backups are dumps, so you can restore to
          the moment a dump ran. PostgreSQL documents point-in-time recovery as part of
          <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/continuous-archiving.html">continuous WAL archiving</a>,
          which you'd set up separately.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You need a cluster or several database nodes.</strong> The one-click database is a single container.
          For multiple nodes or a custom topology, Coolify's docs point to a Docker Compose service or an external
          database provider.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You don't want to run a server at all.</strong> With both self-hosted Coolify and Coolify Cloud, your
          databases run on your own servers. If you'd rather not own server updates, firewalls and restore tests, a
          managed Postgres provider is the trade.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You only need one database and like the terminal.</strong> Plain <code>pg_dump</code> on a cron job
          works. PostgreSQL's docs note that its dumps are internally consistent and don't block other operations while
          they run. Coolify uses the same tool and adds the schedule UI, retention, S3 upload, alerts and a restore
          screen.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Comparing self-hosted platforms? See
        <a href="/compare/coolify-vs-dokploy-vs-caprover-vs-dokku-vs-kamal">Coolify vs Dokploy vs CapRover vs Dokku vs Kamal</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>
    </Prose>

    <div class="mt-4">
      <Faq items={faq} />
    </div>

    <section class="card mt-14 p-6 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-fg">Get started<span class="text-warning">.</span></h2>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-xl text-sm text-fg-dim">
        Install Coolify on your server and deploy your first database. Rather not run the dashboard yourself? Coolify
        Cloud hosts it, and your databases stay on your servers.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
        <a href="https://coolify.io/docs/deploy-your-first-database" class="btn btn-primary">
          <Icon icon={Rocket} class="size-4" />
          Deploy your first database
        </a>
        <a href="/pricing" class="btn btn-neutral">
          <Icon icon={Cloud} class="size-4" />
          Try Coolify Cloud
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
